<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the extension itself. Distinct from the app's, and the
  difference is the whole reason this file exists.

  `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension`, not `packet-tunnel-provider`. The
  plain spelling is what a development-signed build uses and what every iOS
  example shows; a Developer ID build needs the -systemextension variant, and
  Xcode does not make the substitution for you. Signed with the wrong one, the
  extension installs and then fails to start a tunnel — which reads as a bug in
  the tunnel rather than in the signature.

  Nothing else is declared. An entitlement that nothing uses is not free: each one
  has to be enabled on the App ID and present in the provisioning profile, and a
  profile missing one fails the signature or the launch — so an unused app group
  would have been a portal step and a failure mode bought for nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
